---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import nickPhoto from '../assets/nick-noreña.jpeg';

const SCHEDULING_LINK = "https://calendar.app.google/ZgCjgA8Mk3tosTmh9";
---

<MarketingLayout
	title="About | People Purpose Product"
	description="A blog and a practice exploring people, purpose, and products, working alongside mission-driven teams to help the helpers of the world do more good work."
>
	<section class="page-header">
		<h1>About</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			The questions explored in this blog come from real work. <b>People Purpose Product</b>
			is also a practice where I coach and build alongside mission-driven teams.
			We ship products that serve people, and the team's toolbox gets bigger while we
			do it. The purpose is simple: help the helpers of the world have a bigger impact through software.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section team-section">
		<h2>Nick Noreña</h2>
		<div class="person">
			<Image src={nickPhoto} alt="Nick Noreña" width={320} height={320} class="photo" />
			<div class="bio">
				<p class="role">Founder</p>
				<p>
					I've spent my career moving between coaching and product management, always close to the people
					a product serves. I was a founding team member and Head of Coaching at Kromatic, where
					I worked with more than 30 organizations, including Siemens, Unilever, General Mills,
					the US Government, and United Way, teaching innovation tools and coaching teams through
					discovery and validation.
				</p>
				<p>
					As a product manager, I've shipped products that serve people: affordable
					housing platforms for the City of Detroit and the Bay Area, a resource directory
					connecting San Francisco families to critical services, and a worker-owned platform
					advancing economic justice for gig workers with Driver's Seat Cooperative. Most
					recently, I've been leading product on AI tools that help parents navigate government
					benefits programs.
				</p>
				<p>
					Now I'm focused on a narrow question: what happens when mission-driven organizations
					get access to the same tools enterprises have, and use them without losing what makes
					them different?
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section closing" id="work-with-me">
		<h2>Working together</h2>
		<!-- Collaborators stay unnamed by design (employed elsewhere full-time) -->
		<p>
			I build <i>with</i> clients, working side by side to design experiments,
			make product decisions, and ship together. By the time an engagement ends,
			your team understands what was built and why, and can maintain and extend it without
			coming back to me. When a project needs more hands, I bring in a small group of
			experienced collaborators whose judgment I trust.
		</p>
		<p>
			If any of this resonates, I'd love to talk. Book a 30 min consultation call to go over
			what you are working on and how I can help.
		</p>
		<a href={SCHEDULING_LINK} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="cta-button">
			Find a time →
		</a>
		<p class="schedule-note">
			Prefer email? Write me at <a class="email-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/nicknorena/">hello at this domain</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
</MarketingLayout>

<script>
	// Assemble the address in the browser so scrapers reading the raw HTML
	// never see a complete email. The href above is a fallback (LinkedIn)
	// for the rare no-JS visitor.
	const user = 'hello';
	const domain = 'peoplepurposeproduct.com';
	const address = `${user}@${domain}`;
	document.querySelectorAll('.email-link').forEach((el) => {
		el.setAttribute('href', `mailto:${address}`);
		el.textContent = address;
	});
</script>

<style>
	.page-header {
		padding: 3em 0 2.5em;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		margin-bottom: 3em;
	}
	.page-header h1 {
		font-size: 3rem;
		line-height: 1.1;
	}
	.page-header .lede {
		margin-top: 1em;
		max-width: 720px;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}

	.content-section {
		margin-bottom: 3em;
		padding-bottom: 3em;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		max-width: 720px;
	}
	.content-section h2 {
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.75em;
	}
	.closing {
		border-bottom: none;
	}

	/* Team layout: photo floats top-right, bio text starts at the left
	   margin (matching the sections below) and wraps around it */
	.person::after {
		content: "";
		display: block;
		clear: both;
	}
	.photo {
		float: right;
		width: 160px;
		height: 160px;
		margin: 0 0 1em 2em;
		border-radius: 8px;
		object-fit: cover;
	}
	.bio .role {
		font-weight: 700;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.05em;
		margin-bottom: 0.75em;
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: inline-block;
		background: var(--accent);
		color: white;
		padding: 0.75em 1.5em;
		border-radius: 4px;
		text-decoration: none;
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1rem;
		transition: background 0.2s ease;
	}
	.cta-button:hover {
		background: var(--accent-dark);
		color: white;
	}
	.schedule-note {
		margin-top: 1em;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
	.schedule-note a {
		color: var(--accent);
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.page-header h1 {
			font-size: 2.25rem;
		}
		.photo {
			float: none;
			width: 120px;
			height: 120px;
			margin: 0 0 1.5em;
		}
	}
</style>
